+/*
+ * LasecSimul: one virtual clock per vCPU. On the chip the two cores run in parallel: N instructions
+ * take the same time on one core whether the other is busy or idle. Single-threaded TCG runs the
+ * vCPUs in turn, and stock icount charged every instruction of both to the one counter that is the
+ * clock, so each core of a busy pair ran at half speed (measured: a loop took 75.8 ms on one core
+ * alone and 140-151 ms on either core with both busy). Now:
+ *  - each vCPU has its own clock (ns, without qemu_icount_bias), advanced by its own instructions at
+ *    the ps_per_inst in effect when they executed;
+ *  - QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL is that clock inside guest execution (MMIO timestamps, CCOUNT, timer
+ *    reads); everywhere else (timers, bottom halves, the main loop, what the Core is told) it is the
+ *    global clock: the clock of the slowest vCPU that is not halted. Every event a vCPU publishes is
+ *    at or after it, so nothing older can still appear;
+ *  - a halted vCPU (waiti) leaves that minimum and rejoins at the global clock when it runs again;
+ *  - while two vCPUs run, neither may get more than ICOUNT_CPU_QUANTUM_NS ahead of the slowest (see
+ *    icount_cpu_budget): shared memory is visible at once, so this bounds how early or late one core
+ *    can see what the other wrote. Interrupts between cores are exact: raising one kicks the running
+ *    vCPU out, and the woken one rejoins at that instant (icount_cpu_on_timeline);
+ *  - with lockstep, a vCPU ahead of the global clock that needs a sync waits for the others to reach
